{"name":"Skeleton Bone","hex":"#f4ebbc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"skeleton","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The system that provides support to an organism, where in vertebrates it is internal and consists of bones and cartilage, and external in some other animals.","An anthropomorphic representation of a skeleton."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reduce to a skeleton; to skin; to skeletonize.","To minimize."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/skeleton"},{"term":"bone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A composite material consisting largely of calcium phosphate and collagen and making up the skeleton of most vertebrates.","Any of the components of an endoskeleton, made of this material."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an off-white colour, like the colour of bone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Skeleton","blocks":[{"text":"A skeleton is the structural frame that supports the body of most animals. There are several types of skeletons, including the exoskeleton, which is a rigid outer shell that holds up an organism's shape; the endoskeleton, a rigid internal frame to which the organs and soft tissues attach; and the hydroskeleton, a flexible internal structure supported by the hydrostatic pressure of body fluids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Horse_and_Man.jpg/330px-Horse_and_Man.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skeleton"},{"title":"Bone","blocks":[{"text":"A bone is a rigid organ that constitutes part of the skeleton in most vertebrate animals. Bones protect the organs of the body, produce red and white blood cells, store minerals, help regulate acid-base homeostasis, provide structure and support for the body, and enable mobility and hearing. Bones come in a variety of shapes and sizes and have complex internal and external structures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Left_femur_of_extinct_elephant%2C_Alaska%2C_Ice_Age_Wellcome_L0057714.jpg/330px-Left_femur_of_extinct_elephant%2C_Alaska%2C_Ice_Age_Wellcome_L0057714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Hym-myrmicinae.gif/330px-Hym-myrmicinae.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Hym-myrmicinae.gif/330px-Hym-myrmicinae.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Exoskeleton of an ant","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHym-myrmicinae.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Eptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg/500px-Eptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Eptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg/1280px-Eptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Eptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg/500px-Eptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Endoskeleton of a bat","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEptesicus_fuscus_skeleton.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Huxley_-_Mans_Place_in_Nature.jpg/500px-Huxley_-_Mans_Place_in_Nature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Huxley_-_Mans_Place_in_Nature.jpg/500px-Huxley_-_Mans_Place_in_Nature.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pithecometra: From Thomas Huxley's 1863 Evidence as to Man's Place in Nature, the compared skeletons of apes to humans.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuxley_-_Mans_Place_in_Nature.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zalophus_californianus_01.JPG/500px-Zalophus_californianus_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zalophus_californianus_01.JPG/1280px-Zalophus_californianus_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Zalophus_californianus_01.JPG/500px-Zalophus_californianus_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Californian sea lion","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZalophus_californianus_01.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Leonardo_Skeleton_1511.jpg/330px-Leonardo_Skeleton_1511.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Leonardo_Skeleton_1511.jpg/330px-Leonardo_Skeleton_1511.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars